docs/sol/sol_round6.out:7:approval: never
docs/sol/sol_round6.out:14:You have reviewed this system eight times. Round 7: conditional approval of the proportionality reset pending four design changes. Round 8: you verified the four changes, passed the claims/benchmark corrections and Decision E's honesty, and left exactly four remaining items — three design changes and one wording change — stating: "Once those are added, I would regard the remaining work — VM escape attempts, credential searches, live-record migration, exact-version binding, branch-protection attacks, spend races, hostile voice tests, and verified removal of the old paths — as build-phase proof rather than further architectural debate."
docs/sol/sol_round6.out:18:1. OWNER-CONTROLLED, CONTINUOUSLY VALID ADMISSION. Only the authenticated owner can create, change, or revoke admissions, the allowlist, connected-tool approvals, or the EMR refusal; these records live outside anything an agent can edit. Admission does not survive drift: the broker re-checks live repository state (automation, webhooks, integrations, publishing settings) against the recorded proof before each action and refuses on mismatch; a proposed change that would itself add deployment/publishing/privileged-automation behavior cancels the repo's admission until the owner re-admits. Repos are identified by GitHub's permanent repository ID. The "owner's tap = manual deploy step" exception now only counts where the tap physically starts the separate deployment and cannot rescue a repo where merge itself deploys.
