docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3037:I would not approve this design to be built as the architecture of record.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3053:| 2 | Release facts written outside the harness | **Addressed only in words** | “The record-writer [is the] sole writer” and the harness submits “authenticated observations” ([release powers](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:260)). This controls who writes rows, not who can manufacture facts. There is no signed evidence from each reviewer, tester, builder, or approver that the record-writer cannot forge. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3054:| 3 | Qualified independent human review | **Addressed only in words** | The distinction between technical inspection and Neill’s business approval is now correct, and high-authority work is said to remain pending ([human roles](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:90)). But “qualified,” “independent,” who appoints the reviewer, how the reviewer authenticates, and how approval is protected from forgery are undefined. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3071:| 1 | Qualified-reviewer mechanics | **Still missing.** The role is declared, but qualification, appointment, independence, authentication, removal, conflicts, review environment, and signed approval are absent. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3073:| 3 | Authenticated owner approval | **Genuinely addressed for stolen-session protection.** A device-bound, short-lived, single-use approval tied to the package and action is the right mechanism. It still fails if the web page itself is compromised and shows Neill one action while asking his device to approve another. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3077:| 7 | Production-side verification | **Genuinely addressed for rejecting unsigned packages.** It does not establish that every other part of the running release—settings, commands, secrets, schedules, side programs—was approved. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3122:3. **Merging before approval.** The document never states that a pending high-authority change cannot enter the shared source branch. The builder later combines other work with that branch.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3125:6. **Restore and recovery.** An old backup may contain an earlier reviewer assignment, approval, authority description, or classifier state. The document says this must not happen but provides no mechanism.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3139:There is also a bootstrapping contradiction. The classifier, signer, record-writer, production verifier, approval service, and containment policies are themselves high-authority. With the reviewer seat empty, the first trustworthy installation of this system cannot be approved. The document needs to say plainly that work may be prepared in isolation, but the new security system cannot become trusted or control production until a qualified reviewer has inspected the initial implementation.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3147:| Merge caller | Merge arbitrary code into the shared source and contaminate every later build. The document does not require it to verify technical approval before merging. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3149:| Signer | Sign an unapproved or withdrawn package. Since production’s main test is the signature, the signer can create what production treats as valid. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3152:| Record-writer | Append false reviews, approvals, evidence claims, or release status. An append-only store stops erasure; it does not stop the authorized writer from appending lies. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3165:- the required human and model approvals;
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3201:| Hooks and build steps | A builder may execute hostile installation or generation behavior before human approval. Its exact network access, disposable environment, and store access are not defined. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3205:| Voice | The commit action remains undefined. Ambient speech cannot approve a dangerous release, but it can still create work, flood queues, and consume money. |
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3226:4. **Reviewer governance.** Qualification, appointment, identity, hardware-backed approval, independence, conflicts, replacement, revocation, and safe review environment.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3229:7. **The complete release state machine.** The exact order of classification, build, review, approval, merge, rebuild or verification, signing, rollout, and final release.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3237:15. **A safe transition plan.** The old release system must be disabled before it can build, approve, or deploy the new security system.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3242:No, I would not approve this design to be built.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3254:4. **Write the release state machine.** Specify the exact order from patch intake to production. No step may rely on a later step to repair an earlier unsafe action. In particular, clarify when merging occurs and how a moving shared source invalidates approvals.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3268:11. **Protect owner and reviewer transactions from a compromised web page.** The trusted device must display or independently verify the actual package and action being approved. A server-chosen passkey challenge is insufficient by itself.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3289:- Technical-review and owner approvals cannot be forged, replayed, swapped, or applied after the source changes.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3290:- External flags and schedules cannot activate unapproved behavior.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3293:- Restoring a backup cannot revive credentials, approvals, identities, packages, automation, or release paths that were withdrawn.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:3301:I would not approve this design to be built as the architecture of record.
