docs/sol/sol_task10_gauntlet.out:3007:5. Correct. A current-version fail is not outvotable; it blocks both `done` and `shipped` ([schema.sql:874](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:874)). Verdicts cannot change except `needs_human → pass|fail` ([schema.sql:685](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:685)). Adding a tiebreaker pass does nothing to the original fail. A contested version must be revised into a new version, not “won” by escalation. Escalation makes sense for uncertainty represented as `needs_human`, provided the design resolves that exact row through the existing resolution path ([jobs.py:326](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:326)).
docs/sol/sol_task10_gauntlet.out:3039:1. The panel has no integration point, while the old single-review path remains capable of attempting completion. More broadly, the full-panel guard runs only for transitions to `done`, not direct transitions to `shipped` ([schema.sql:850](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:850)). `ship()` does not require the prior state to be `done` ([dispatch.py:250](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:250)). That is an existing database-level bypass of the reviewer panel.
docs/sol/sol_task10_gauntlet.out:3065:Residual risks remain even after these adjustments: the database cannot prove that the bytes shown to a remote model correspond to the recorded hash, provider identity is trusted through the runner, and task #11’s eventual gatekeeper must independently verify that the code leaving the system is exactly the reviewed version.
docs/sol/sol_task10_gauntlet.out:3080:5. Correct. A current-version fail is not outvotable; it blocks both `done` and `shipped` ([schema.sql:874](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:874)). Verdicts cannot change except `needs_human → pass|fail` ([schema.sql:685](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:685)). Adding a tiebreaker pass does nothing to the original fail. A contested version must be revised into a new version, not “won” by escalation. Escalation makes sense for uncertainty represented as `needs_human`, provided the design resolves that exact row through the existing resolution path ([jobs.py:326](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:326)).
docs/sol/sol_task10_gauntlet.out:3112:1. The panel has no integration point, while the old single-review path remains capable of attempting completion. More broadly, the full-panel guard runs only for transitions to `done`, not direct transitions to `shipped` ([schema.sql:850](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:850)). `ship()` does not require the prior state to be `done` ([dispatch.py:250](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:250)). That is an existing database-level bypass of the reviewer panel.
docs/sol/sol_task10_gauntlet.out:3138:Residual risks remain even after these adjustments: the database cannot prove that the bytes shown to a remote model correspond to the recorded hash, provider identity is trusted through the runner, and task #11’s eventual gatekeeper must independently verify that the code leaving the system is exactly the reviewed version.
