docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6681:docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:138:| **Scheduled / overnight jobs** (OpenClaw scheduler) | They run *unattended*, so a skipped check has no human nearby. | Overnight jobs get the *most* restricted profile: local/subscription seats only, no dangerous capability can be requested while unattended (approvals require a live read-back, which needs the owner awake — see §6). Worst case overnight is wasted tokens and code sitting in a branch for morning review. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6683:docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:140:| **The gatekeeper / release service itself** | It is the one thing that *must* act. | It is the smallest, most-reviewed component, runs as a separate trusted user, authenticates its callers, and re-verifies every request against records only it can write (§4). It is the single trusted computing base — kept tiny on purpose. |
