docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:9933:OpenClaw’s own documentation says sandboxing is off by default. It also says that without a sandbox, work runs on the host computer, with no approval required by default. [OpenClaw sandboxing](https://docs.openclaw.ai/gateway/sandboxing), [OpenClaw execution controls](https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/exec).
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:9937:A database permission can become meaningful only when a separate, trusted gatekeeper has the production credentials and agents do not. The agent asks that gatekeeper to act; the gatekeeper checks the approval, performs exactly the approved action once, and rejects everything else. The real containment comes from credentials, network separation, restricted files, and that gatekeeper—not from the database row.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:9989:Reviews are not tied to an exact version of the work. A reviewer can approve version A, the builder can change it into version B, and the old approval still counts.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10003:There is no trusted executor that consumes an approval and performs the bound action. The “what was read aloud” field is merely text somebody entered; it is not proof of what Neill actually heard.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10005:I also proved that an approval can be created already granted while bypassing the recovery-plan check.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10007:Finally, the mouth paraphrases Chief’s response. Therefore the database may record one exact warning while Neill hears different wording. That destroys the claimed connection between “what was read back” and “what he approved.”
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10060:- Safe voice approval of dangerous actions while driving: no. Do not allow it.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10062:For dangerous actions, Chief should explain the consequence aloud, but approval should wait until Neill is stopped and taps a clearly written confirmation.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10068:- A trusted action gatekeeper holding all dangerous credentials.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10130:OpenClaw’s own documentation says sandboxing is off by default. It also says that without a sandbox, work runs on the host computer, with no approval required by default. [OpenClaw sandboxing](https://docs.openclaw.ai/gateway/sandboxing), [OpenClaw execution controls](https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/exec).
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10134:A database permission can become meaningful only when a separate, trusted gatekeeper has the production credentials and agents do not. The agent asks that gatekeeper to act; the gatekeeper checks the approval, performs exactly the approved action once, and rejects everything else. The real containment comes from credentials, network separation, restricted files, and that gatekeeper—not from the database row.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10186:Reviews are not tied to an exact version of the work. A reviewer can approve version A, the builder can change it into version B, and the old approval still counts.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10200:There is no trusted executor that consumes an approval and performs the bound action. The “what was read aloud” field is merely text somebody entered; it is not proof of what Neill actually heard.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10202:I also proved that an approval can be created already granted while bypassing the recovery-plan check.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10204:Finally, the mouth paraphrases Chief’s response. Therefore the database may record one exact warning while Neill hears different wording. That destroys the claimed connection between “what was read back” and “what he approved.”
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10257:- Safe voice approval of dangerous actions while driving: no. Do not allow it.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10259:For dangerous actions, Chief should explain the consequence aloud, but approval should wait until Neill is stopped and taps a clearly written confirmation.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:10265:- A trusted action gatekeeper holding all dangerous credentials.
docs/sol/sol_out.txt:7:approval: never
docs/sol/sol_out.txt:41:A table of what each agent may do. Safe: read code, write in own worktree, run tests, open a PR. Denied by default: touch production, delete data, deploy, merge to main, force push, read secrets, send external requests, spend money. Anything irreversible needs a single-use approval naming the exact action, with what was READ BACK ALOUD to Neill, and a recovery plan.
