docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4012:I checked the current setup. All agents share the same live project area. No effective restrictions or safety extension are configured. Worse, OpenClaw says that when command restrictions are not configured, direct access on the host is unrestricted by default. [OpenClaw’s command-approval documentation](https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/exec-approvals) confirms that default.
docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4020:OpenClaw does provide the pieces needed to fix this. It supports separate restrictions for each agent, isolated work areas, host-level command approvals, and extensions that can block an action before it runs. It even identifies budget enforcement as an intended use for its trusted pre-action policies. [Its extension documentation](https://docs.openclaw.ai/plugins/hooks) and [per-agent restriction documentation](https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/multi-agent-sandbox-tools) make the hybrid technically credible.
docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4056:Use OpenClaw for background dispatch, parallel agents, sessions, memory, scheduling, recovery, and the normal interface. Keep the custom database for permanent job history, review decisions, evidence, approvals, and budget reservations. Move the enforcement into one Chief Command safety extension that OpenClaw calls before work starts and before every consequential action.
docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4074:Then build an attack test for every agent type and every OpenClaw upgrade. The test should deliberately try to bypass the job record, forge evidence, change the protected version, spend beyond the limit, reach production, alter the rulebook, and reuse an approval. If any runner can do one of those things, that runner remains disabled until the route is closed.
docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4095:I checked the current setup. All agents share the same live project area. No effective restrictions or safety extension are configured. Worse, OpenClaw says that when command restrictions are not configured, direct access on the host is unrestricted by default. [OpenClaw’s command-approval documentation](https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/exec-approvals) confirms that default.
docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4103:OpenClaw does provide the pieces needed to fix this. It supports separate restrictions for each agent, isolated work areas, host-level command approvals, and extensions that can block an action before it runs. It even identifies budget enforcement as an intended use for its trusted pre-action policies. [Its extension documentation](https://docs.openclaw.ai/plugins/hooks) and [per-agent restriction documentation](https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/multi-agent-sandbox-tools) make the hybrid technically credible.
