docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3775:The existing system confirms that the document is describing a replacement, not today’s protection. Today, the general harness can write reviews and evidence, mark work shipped, launch model runners directly, and mint voice access from a provider key. The current voice path also uses model-based end-of-speech detection and allows paraphrasing. Those old routes are not merely unfinished; during the rebuild they must be removed or made impossible to run, or they become bypasses around the new design.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3781:  ✓ Deliver a plain-English approval verdict with ranked conditions
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3802:| 2. Release service owns release facts | **Addressed only in words** | “Verdicts, approvals, evidence provenance, and released status are written only by the release service” carries the requirement ([release facts](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:152)). But the harness submits “authenticated observations.” If the whole harness shares one submitting identity, a compromised harness can submit false reviewer and test observations, and the release service will faithfully turn them into trusted facts. Sole writing does not establish truth. The phrase “or a release-account process” also opens an undefined second writer. |
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3906:- **GitHub itself:** Administrator bypass, automation identities, package publishing, release creation, environment approvals and third-party GitHub applications may provide alternate authority.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3912:- **Backup and restoration:** Restoring old configuration can revive old credentials, stale approvals, unsafe automation or a vulnerable release path.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3914:Production must reject anything not signed by the sole approved chain. Merely saying “all releases should use this service” is not enough.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3928:- Social engineering that persuades Neill to paste a secret, mount a folder or approve a system dialog.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3950:1. **A qualified technical reviewer.** Who can inspect dangerous code independently, and how their approval is tied to the exact package.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3952:3. **Authenticated written approval.** How the system proves Neill—not a stolen session or injected web request—signed, preferably using a device-bound passkey or biometric confirmation.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3980:## 8. Required for approval
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3994:9. **Define authenticated human approval and exact critical-message delivery.** Critical warnings are played from fixed text, not paraphrased. Written approval is device-bound, short-lived, single-use and tied to the exact package and action.
