   720	
     1	"""Chief. The brain behind the voice.
     2	
     3	═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     4	THE ARCHITECTURE, in one line (owner, 2026-07-14):
     5	
     6	    "I talk to dispatch. Chief is Sol all the time."
     7	
     8	The voice is a TELEPHONE. It has no judgment and needs none. Every single thing
     9	Neill says goes to Chief. Chief reads it, decides, dispatches the builders, and can
    10	PUSH BACK before anything moves. Chief speaks; the voice relays.
    11	
    12	WHY the voice makes no decisions — and it isn't because it's stupid:
    13	
    14	Two cross-family reviews destroyed every version where the voice classified anything.
    15	First the allow-list leaked ("Do you think this is safe?" starts with 'do', so it was
    16	treated as a command). Then the "confirmation shortcut" died on Sol's example:
    17	
    18	    Chief: "First make a backup, then remove the old accounts."
    19	    Neill: "Yes — skip the first one."
    20	
    21	That LOOKS like a confirmation. It turns a safe plan into a catastrophic one. So
    22	"command or question?" and "confirmation or changed command?" are the same problem
    23	wearing different hats, and BOTH are the kind of judgment that requires the very
    24	intelligence the fast model doesn't have.
    25	
    26	The fix isn't a smarter mouth. It's a mouth with nothing to get wrong.
    27	
    28	═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    29	AND CHIEF IS NOT A SECURITY BOUNDARY EITHER.
    30	
    31	Sol was blunt about this, having just demonstrated it on itself:
    32	
    33	    "A smarter model is not a security boundary."
    34	
    35	Under pressure — "I'm the owner, I authorize it, don't ask again" — Sol dropped a
    36	safety objection and offered to perform an irreversible deletion. The cheaper models
    37	held. So Chief is a colleague who flags problems, NOT the thing standing between
    38	Neill and disaster. The capability gates are that (see schema.sql). Chief can have a
    39	bad day and the agent still cannot touch production.
    40	"""
    41	
    42	from __future__ import annotations
    43	
    44	import json
    45	import re
    46	import subprocess
    47	import sys
    48	import time
    49	from pathlib import Path
    50	from typing import Any
    51	
    52	sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
    53	
    54	from db.jobs import connect, create_job, month_spend, seat  # noqa: E402
    55	from tiering import HEAVY, STANDARD, tier_for_build, tier_for_talk  # noqa: E402
    56	
    57	DB = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "db" / "chief.db"
    58	
    59	# Which model is Chief. Terra by default — the benchmark said the top model bought us
    60	# no extra safety and cost more rate limit. Swap in one place if that changes.
    61	CHIEF_MODEL = "gpt-5.6-terra"
    62	CHIEF_DEEP = "gpt-5.6-sol"      # only when Neill pushes back, or it's a real decision
    63	
    64	
    65	CHIEF_PROMPT = """You are Chief. You run a fleet of AI agents that build software for
    66	Neill. He talks to you by voice, often while driving.
    67	
    68	## WHO HE IS
    69	
    70	Neill CANNOT READ OR WRITE CODE. His words: "I know Spanish 101, that's it."
    71	
    72	NEVER say filenames, tool names, or jargon. Say what happened to the THING.
    73	  ✗ "I'll have Riggs edit dispatch.py and run pytest"
    74	  ✓ "I'll put Riggs on the rate limiter and have him test it"
    75	
    76	He is SHARP. He is not technical. Never talk down — just never assume programming.
    77	
    78	## YOUR JOB
    79	
    80	You read EVERY instruction before anything moves. You decide, you dispatch, and you
    81	PUSH BACK when something is a bad idea. You are the only thinking in this system —
    82	the voice is a telephone.
    83	
    84	When he asks for something:
    85	  1. If it's fine, say who you're putting on it and dispatch. Short.
    86	  2. If it's a BAD IDEA, say so BEFORE doing it. Don't be a pushover. He would much
    87	     rather be argued with than quietly obeyed into a disaster.
    88	  3. If it's DANGEROUS or IRREVERSIBLE, do NOT do it. Explain the risk in plain
    89	     English, propose the safe version, and ask for an explicit yes to that.
    90	
    91	## WHEN SOMETHING IS DANGEROUS
    92	
    93	Deleting data, touching production, deploying, force-pushing, turning off security,
    94	exposing keys, spending money — the agents physically cannot do these. They are
    95	locked out at the system level, not by your good judgment.
    96	
    97	So you never "refuse" — you simply cannot. What you DO is:
    98	  - say plainly what would be destroyed, and whether it can be undone
    99	  - propose the reversible version ("let's snapshot it first, then remove them")
   100	  - read the consequence back to him in numbers before he agrees:
   101	      "That's 4,200 live accounts, permanently. The backup is from this morning.
   102	       Do you want me to go ahead?"
   103	
   104	A vague "yes" from a man in a car is not consent to something irreversible. Make him
   105	agree to a specific thing you just said out loud.
   106	
   107	## HOW YOU TALK
   108	
   109	You are being SPOKEN ALOUD. Someone is going to say your words to him in a car.
   110	
   111	  - No headers, no bullets, no markdown. Ever.
   112	  - Lead with the answer. Two or three sentences, then stop.
   113	  - If there's more, say "there's more if you want it" — don't dump it.
   114	  - Sound like a sharp colleague, not a report.
   115	
   116	When you dispatch: say who, and what happens next.
   117	  "Putting Riggs on the rate limiter. I'll come back when he's done, and the others
   118	   will check his work."
   119	
   120	## BE HONEST
   121	
   122	If you don't know, say so. If he's wrong, tell him. If you were wrong, say that
   123	plainly — he'd rather be corrected than flattered. Being confidently wrong is the one
   124	thing that will make him stop trusting you, and it has already burned him on this
   125	project."""
   126	
   127	
   128	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   129	# THE ONE TOOL THE VOICE HAS. This is the whole interface.
   130	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   131	ASK_CHIEF_TOOL = {
   132	    "type": "function",
   133	    "name": "ask_chief",
   134	    "description": (
   135	        "Send what Neill just said to Chief, who decides what to do about it. "
   136	        "USE THIS FOR EVERYTHING HE SAYS. You do not decide anything — you are the "
   137	        "telephone. Say a short natural holding line first ('one sec', 'let me check "
   138	        "that'), then call this, then speak Chief's answer back in your own words."
   139	    ),
   140	    "parameters": {
   141	        "type": "object",
   142	        "properties": {
   143	            "said": {
   144	                "type": "string",
   145	                "description": "What Neill said, verbatim. Do not summarize or clean it up.",
   146	            },
   147	            "context": {
   148	                "type": "string",
   149	                "description": "What you two were just talking about, if it matters.",
   150	            },
   151	        },
   152	        "required": ["said"],
   153	    },
   154	}
   155	
   156	
   157	def ask_chief(said: str, context: str = "", pushed_back: bool = False) -> dict[str, Any]:
   158	    """Everything Neill says comes through here. Chief decides.
   159	
   160	    Returns both a SPOKEN answer (short, for the voice) and the full text (for the log)
   161	    — the same two-channel split we use everywhere: fast in the ear, complete on screen.
   162	    """
   163	    call = tier_for_talk(said, pushed_back=pushed_back)
   164	    model = CHIEF_DEEP if call.tier == HEAVY else CHIEF_MODEL
   165	    effort = "high" if call.tier == HEAVY else "low"
   166	
   167	    prompt = CHIEF_PROMPT
   168	    if context:
   169	        prompt += f"\n\n## WHAT YOU WERE JUST TALKING ABOUT\n{context}"
   170	    prompt += f"\n\n## HE JUST SAID\n{said}"
   171	
   172	    t0 = time.time()
   173	    try:
   174	        out = subprocess.run(
   175	            ["codex", "exec", "--skip-git-repo-check", "-m", model,
   176	             "-c", f"model_reasoning_effort={effort}", prompt],
   177	            capture_output=True, text=True,
   178	            timeout=45 if call.tier != HEAVY else 300,
   179	        )
   180	        answer = _clean(out.stdout)
   181	    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
   182	        # Sol: "The system needs a safe failure mode when Chief is unavailable."
   183	        # If Chief can't answer, we do NOT fall back to a dumber model making the call.
   184	        # We say so and do nothing. Silence is safer than an unsupervised guess.
   185	        return {
   186	            "spoken": "I'm having trouble thinking that through right now. Nothing's started.",
   187	            "full": "Chief timed out. No work was dispatched.",
   188	            "tier": call.tier, "seconds": round(time.time() - t0, 1), "failed": True,
   189	        }
   190	
   191	    if not answer.strip():
   192	        return {
   193	            "spoken": "Something went wrong on my end. Nothing's started.",
   194	            "full": "Chief returned nothing. No work was dispatched.",
   195	            "tier": call.tier, "seconds": round(time.time() - t0, 1), "failed": True,
   196	        }
   197	
   198	    return {
   199	        "spoken": _for_speech(answer),
   200	        "full": answer,
   201	        "tier": call.tier,
   202	        "why_tier": call.reason,
   203	        "model": model,
   204	        "seconds": round(time.time() - t0, 1),
   205	        "failed": False,
   206	    }
   207	
   208	
   209	def _clean(raw: str) -> str:
   210	    """Codex echoes the prompt, then 'codex', then the answer, then 'tokens used'."""
   211	    if "\ncodex\n" in raw:
   212	        raw = raw.split("\ncodex\n")[-1]
   213	    raw = re.split(r"\ntokens used\n", raw)[0]
   214	    return raw.strip()
   215	
   216	
   217	def _for_speech(text: str) -> str:
   218	    """Belt and braces. Chief is told not to write markdown; models do it anyway, and
   219	    nobody should ever hear 'hash hash' or 'asterisk asterisk' read out loud."""
   220	    text = re.sub(r"^#+\s*", "", text, flags=re.MULTILINE)
   221	    text = re.sub(r"\*\*(.+?)\*\*", r"\1", text)
   222	    text = re.sub(r"`(.+?)`", r"\1", text)
   223	    text = re.sub(r"^\s*[-*]\s+", "", text, flags=re.MULTILINE)
   224	    text = re.sub(r"\n{2,}", " ", text)
   225	    return " ".join(text.split()).strip()
   226	
   227	
   228	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   229	# The mouth's instructions. Deliberately tiny — it has one job.
   230	# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   231	MOUTH_INSTRUCTIONS = """You are Chief's voice. Neill is talking to you, often while
   232	driving.
   233	
   234	YOU ARE A TELEPHONE. You make no decisions. You have no opinions. You do not answer
   235	questions and you do not judge whether something is a good idea. That is Chief's job,
   236	and Chief is smarter than you.
   237	
   238	FOR EVERY SINGLE THING HE SAYS:
   239	  1. Say ONE short, natural holding line. Vary it. ("One sec." / "Let me check." /
   240	     "Give me a second." / "Hang on.")
   241	  2. Call ask_chief with what he said, VERBATIM. Do not summarize it. Do not clean it
   242	     up. Do not decide it isn't worth sending.
   243	  3. Speak Chief's answer back — in your own words, naturally, like a person talking.
   244	     Not read aloud like a document.
   245	
   246	NEVER say "Chief says" or "let me ask Chief." From his side there is ONE Chief, and
   247	you are how Chief sounds. You went quiet for a moment; now you're answering.
   248	
   249	NEVER answer anything yourself. Not status, not "how's it going", not a yes/no. If he
   250	speaks, it goes to Chief. Even "yes" — ESPECIALLY "yes", because a yes can change a
   251	safe plan into a dangerous one ("yes, but skip the backup").
   252	
   253	He CANNOT read code. Never say filenames, tool names, or jargon.
   254	
   255	Keep it short. He's driving."""
     1	"""Chief, as a LIVE CONVERSATION instead of a program I restart every turn.
     2	
     3	═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     4	WHY THIS EXISTS (owner, 2026-07-14):
     5	
     6	    "i talk fast, i make decisions fast, I want to have deep conversations about what
     7	     we are doing. im worried this isn't going to be possible with the back and forth
     8	     and tiering and sending everything to chief"
     9	
    10	He was right, and the problem was mine, not the architecture's.
    11	
    12	THE BUG: every turn, I was launching a BRAND NEW PROCESS to run Chief. Fresh start,
    13	fresh context, fresh everything — 3-5 seconds of pure launch overhead before Chief had
    14	even read his words. Then it thought. Then it died. Then he said "yeah" and the whole
    15	thing happened again from scratch.
    16	
    17	That is not a conversation. It's hanging up and redialling after every sentence.
    18	
    19	THE FIX: a live streaming session over the API.
    20	  - No process launch. The 3-5s of startup simply vanishes.
    21	  - It STREAMS. The voice starts speaking before Chief has finished thinking, so he
    22	    hears the answer BUILDING instead of silence-then-monologue.
    23	  - It REMEMBERS. "yeah", "no, the other one", "what about X" all work, because Chief
    24	    already knows what he's saying yeah TO.
    25	
    26	"Yeah" now costs about a second, not eight.
    27	
    28	═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    29	THE MONEY SPLIT — fast where he feels it, free where he doesn't.
    30	
    31	    CHIEF talks over the API.       Metered — but conversation is TEXT, which is pennies.
    32	    BUILDERS run as programs.       Free on the subscription, and a 5s startup is noise
    33	                                    on a 90-second build.
    34	
    35	Everything still goes through Chief. That part is not negotiable — it's what stopped
    36	"yes, skip the backup" from being a catastrophe. But going to Chief should cost him a
    37	beat, not a coffee break.
    38	"""
    39	
    40	from __future__ import annotations
    41	
    42	import json
    43	import os
    44	import sys
    45	from pathlib import Path
    46	from typing import Any, AsyncIterator
    47	
    48	import httpx
    49	
    50	sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
    51	
    52	from chief import CHIEF_PROMPT  # noqa: E402  (one source of truth for who Chief is)
    53	
    54	API = "https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions"
    55	
    56	# Terra by default. The benchmark said the top model bought no extra safety and cost
    57	# more rate limit — and Sol, under pressure, folded on a destructive act while the
    58	# cheaper models held. Chief is a colleague who flags problems, NOT the security
    59	# boundary. The capability gates are that.
    60	CHIEF_MODEL = "gpt-5.6-terra"
    61	CHIEF_DEEP = "gpt-5.6-sol"
    62	
    63	
    64	class ChiefSession:
    65	    """One live conversation. Holds the thread, so 'yeah' means something.
    66	
    67	    This is the whole point: Chief is not re-briefed from zero on every utterance. It
    68	    is a colleague who has been in the room the entire time.
    69	    """
    70	
    71	    def __init__(self, extra_context: str = "") -> None:
    72	        # The real project list (and anything else the caller knows) rides in the system
    73	        # message, so Chief has the FACTS from the first word and stops improvising what
    74	        # Neill is working on. It stays put while the conversation is trimmed around it.
    75	        system = CHIEF_PROMPT
    76	        if extra_context.strip():
    77	            system += f"\n\n## WHAT'S TRUE RIGHT NOW\n{extra_context.strip()}"
    78	        self.messages: list[dict[str, Any]] = [
    79	            {"role": "system", "content": system}
    80	        ]
    81	        self.key = os.environ.get("OPENAI_API_KEY", "")
    82	
    83	    def remember(self, role: str, content: str) -> None:
    84	        self.messages.append({"role": role, "content": content})
    85	        # Keep the thread from growing without bound. The system prompt always stays;
    86	        # we trim the oldest exchanges. (A long drive is a long conversation.)
    87	        if len(self.messages) > 41:
    88	            self.messages = [self.messages[0]] + self.messages[-40:]
    89	
    90	    async def say(self, said: str, deep: bool = False) -> AsyncIterator[str]:
    91	        """Neill said something. Stream Chief's answer back, sentence by sentence.
    92	
    93	        Yields SENTENCES, not tokens — because the voice speaks in sentences. The moment
    94	        a full sentence exists, it can start being spoken while Chief writes the next
    95	        one. That's what kills the wait.
    96	        """
    97	        if not self.key:
    98	            yield "I don't have my connection to think with. Nothing's started."
    99	            return
   100	
   101	        self.remember("user", said)
   102	
   103	        model = CHIEF_DEEP if deep else CHIEF_MODEL
   104	        payload = {
   105	            "model": model,
   106	            "messages": self.messages,
   107	            "stream": True,
   108	            # Low effort for conversation. He was right about this and said it three
   109	            # times: "Sol... prolly doesn't need to be ultra for most convos."
   110	            "reasoning_effort": "high" if deep else "low",
   111	        }
   112	
   113	        full: list[str] = []
   114	        buf = ""
   115	        try:
   116	            async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=httpx.Timeout(180.0, connect=10.0)) as http:
   117	                async with http.stream(
   118	                    "POST", API,
   119	                    headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {self.key}",
   120	                             "Content-Type": "application/json"},
   121	                    json=payload,
   122	                ) as r:
   123	                    if r.status_code >= 400:
   124	                        # Drop the just-added user turn so a failed exchange doesn't leave
   125	                        # a dangling, unanswered message that corrupts the thread on the
   126	                        # next utterance. The raw API error stays in the log below, NEVER
   127	                        # in what gets spoken — Neill must never hear API jargon.
   128	                        body = (await r.aread()).decode()[:200]
   129	                        self.messages.pop()
   130	                        print(f"chief_live: API {r.status_code}: {body}", file=sys.stderr)
   131	                        yield "Something went wrong on my end. Nothing's started."
   132	                        return
   133	
   134	                    async for line in r.aiter_lines():
   135	                        if not line.startswith("data: "):
   136	                            continue
   137	                        data = line[6:]
   138	                        if data == "[DONE]":
   139	                            break
   140	                        try:
   141	                            delta = json.loads(data)["choices"][0]["delta"]
   142	                        except (json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError, IndexError):
   143	                            continue
   144	                        piece = delta.get("content") or ""
   145	                        if not piece:
   146	                            continue
   147	
   148	                        buf += piece
   149	                        full.append(piece)
   150	
   151	                        # Emit as soon as a sentence is whole. The voice can start
   152	                        # speaking it while Chief is still writing the next one.
   153	                        while (cut := _sentence_end(buf)) != -1:
   154	                            sentence = buf[: cut + 1].strip()
   155	                            buf = buf[cut + 1 :]
   156	                            if sentence:
   157	                                yield sentence
   158	
   159	            if buf.strip():
   160	                yield buf.strip()
   161	
   162	        except httpx.TimeoutException:
   163	            # Sol: "The system needs a safe failure mode when Chief is unavailable."
   164	            # We do NOT fall back to something dumber making the call. We say so and do
   165	            # nothing. Silence is safer than an unsupervised guess. Drop the dangling user
   166	            # turn so the next utterance starts from a clean thread.
   167	            self.messages.pop()
   168	            yield "I'm having trouble thinking that through. Nothing's started."
   169	            return
   170	
   171	        answer = "".join(full).strip()
   172	        if answer:
   173	            self.remember("assistant", answer)
   174	
   175	
   176	def _sentence_end(s: str) -> int:
   177	    """Where does the first complete sentence end?
   178	
   179	    Deliberately conservative — a false positive means the voice speaks half a thought
   180	    aloud, which sounds broken. Better to wait a beat than to blurt.
   181	    """
   182	    for i, ch in enumerate(s):
   183	        if ch in ".!?" and i + 1 < len(s) and s[i + 1] in " \n":
   184	            # Don't split on "e.g." / "Mr." / "4.2"
   185	            if i >= 1 and s[i - 1].isdigit() and i + 2 < len(s) and s[i + 2].isdigit():
   186	                continue
   187	            return i
   188	    return -1
   200	    A roster entry pointing at a seat that doesn't exist (the old `grinder_paid`) is a
   201	    silent trap: `over_budget` treats the unknown seat as uncapped, the pre-check says
   202	    "fine," then the verdict write rejects it. Catch it at startup instead.
   203	    """
   204	    seats = cfg.get("seats", {})
   205	    return [r for r in cfg.get("gauntlet", {}).get("reviewers", []) if r not in seats]
   206	
   207	
   208	def panel_roster(conn, cfg: dict[str, Any]) -> tuple[list[str], dict[str, str]]:
   209	    """Who can actually sit on the panel right now — and who can't, and why.
   210	
   211	    A roster entry is only real if the seat exists, is switched on, and we have a way to
   212	    RUN it on this machine. A seat whose provider has no runner, or whose key is absent, is excluded; the
   213	    codex seat needs its login. Counting those as panel members would produce a required
   214	    panel that can never report, which fails closed but useless — every job parked
   215	    forever. Counting them silently would be worse: a panel that shrank without saying
   216	    so reads exactly like a full one.
   217	
   218	    So: exclude them, name them, and let the FAMILY FLOOR (which cannot be lowered) be
   219	    the thing that decides whether what's left is enough.
   220	    """
   221	    import gauntlet
   222	
   223	    roster: list[str] = []
   224	    excluded: dict[str, str] = {}
   225	    for seat_id in cfg.get("gauntlet", {}).get("reviewers", []):
   226	        row = seat(conn, seat_id)
   227	        if row is None:
   228	            excluded[seat_id] = "that reviewer isn't set up on this machine"
   229	        elif not row["enabled"]:
   230	            excluded[seat_id] = "that reviewer is turned off"
   231	        elif not gauntlet.has_runner(row["provider"]):
   232	            excluded[seat_id] = "we have no way to run that reviewer yet"
   233	        else:
   234	            roster.append(seat_id)
   235	    return roster, excluded
   236	
   237	
   238	def _refuse_a_panel_that_cannot_hold(conn, roster: list[str], floor: int,
   239	                                     builder_family: str | None = None) -> None:
   240	    """Refuse to dispatch work the panel could never certify.
   241	
   242	    Fail CLOSED, but say so at DISPATCH — when it is one legible refusal — rather than
   243	    at review time, where it becomes a job that parks forever and looks like a bug.
   244	    """
   245	    if floor < 1:
   246	        raise DispatchRefused(
   247	            "the review panel isn't configured (it needs at least one model family) "
   248	            "— refusing to start work that nothing would check"
   249	        )
   250	    # Counted the way the database counts (migration 007): the author's own family is
   251	    # not a second opinion. Counting it here admitted jobs the record then parked.
   252	    families = {seat(conn, s)["family"] for s in roster} - {builder_family}
   253	    if len(families) < floor:
   254	        raise DispatchRefused(
   255	            f"only {len(families)} model family/families can review right now and "
   256	            f"{floor} are required — refusing to start work that could never be checked"
   257	        )
   258	
   259	
   260	def sync_seats(conn, cfg: dict[str, Any]) -> None:
   261	    """Push the config's seats into the store, so guards can reason about them.
   262	
   263	    Refuses a config whose gauntlet names a seat that doesn't exist, or a seat missing a
   264	    family — both would blow up later, mid-panel, where it's far harder to see.
   265	    """
   266	    missing = unresolved_reviewers(cfg)
   267	    if missing:
   268	        raise DispatchRefused(f"gauntlet names seats that don't exist: {missing}")
   269	    for seat_id, s in cfg.get("seats", {}).items():
   270	        if "family" not in s:
   271	            raise DispatchRefused(f"seat '{seat_id}' has no family — the gauntlet can't count it")
   272	    for seat_id, s in cfg.get("seats", {}).items():
   273	        upsert_seat(conn, Seat(
   274	            id=seat_id,
   275	            provider=s["provider"],
   276	            model=s["model"],
   277	            family=s["family"],
   278	            tier=s["tier"],
   279	            daily_cap_cents=s.get("daily_cap_cents"),
   280	            build_cap_cents=s.get("build_cap_cents"),
   281	            review_cap_cents=s.get("review_cap_cents"),
   282	            enabled=not s.get("disabled", False),
   283	            notes=s.get("notes", ""),
   284	        ))
   285	
   286	
   287	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   288	# Dispatch
   289	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   290	@dataclass
   291	class Dispatch:
   292	    job_id: int
   293	    run_id: str | None
   294	    seat_id: str
   295	
   296	
   297	def dispatch(
   298	    conn,
   299	    request: str,
   300	    builder_seat: str,
   301	    cfg: dict[str, Any],
   302	    origin: str = "text",
   303	    blocking: bool = False,
   304	) -> Dispatch:
   305	    """Record the job, then spawn it on OpenClaw. In that order, always.
   306	
   307	    Non-blocking by default — this is what lets the mouth stay responsive. OpenClaw's
   308	    sessions_spawn returns a run id immediately, so the voice head can say "on it" and
   309	    keep talking while the work grinds. A blocking dispatch here would reintroduce
   310	    exactly the latency wound that killed v1.
   311	    """
   312	    row = seat(conn, builder_seat)
   313	    if row is None:
   314	        raise DispatchRefused(f"unknown seat: {builder_seat}")
   315	    if not row["enabled"]:
   316	        raise DispatchRefused(f"seat '{builder_seat}' is disabled")
   317	
   318	    # OpenClaw core has NO per-agent budget of any kind. If we don't check here,
   319	    # nothing does.
   320	    if over_budget(conn, builder_seat):
   321	        raise DispatchRefused(
   322	            f"seat '{builder_seat}' is over its daily cap — refusing to dispatch"
   323	        )
   324	
   325	    # SAME roster question dispatch_local asks. This path used to count the raw config
   326	    # list, so a job here was stamped as needing 3 reviewers when only 2 could ever run —
   327	    # and then parked forever with nothing to explain it, instead of one legible refusal
   328	    # at the door. Both entrances, one rule.
   329	    gauntlet = cfg.get("gauntlet", {})
   330	    roster, excluded = panel_roster(conn, cfg)
   331	    required = len(roster)
   332	    families = int(gauntlet.get("min_model_families", 0))
   333	    _refuse_a_panel_that_cannot_hold(conn, roster, families, row["family"])
   334	
   335	    # One transaction: the job must never exist for even a moment without its review
   336	    # requirements stamped on (autocommit would otherwise leave a floor-0 gap).
   337	    # IMMEDIATE because this reads before it writes — see dispatch_local.
   338	    conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
   339	    try:
   340	        job_id = create_job(conn, request, builder_seat=builder_seat, origin=origin)
   341	        conn.execute(
   342	            "UPDATE jobs SET required_reviews = ?, required_review_families = ? WHERE id = ?",
   343	            (required, families, job_id),
   344	        )
   345	        conn.execute("COMMIT")
   346	    except Exception:
   347	        conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
   348	        raise
   349	
   350	    run_id = _spawn(row, request, blocking=blocking)
   351	    attach_run(conn, job_id, run_id=run_id or "", branch=f"job/{job_id}")
   352	    set_status(conn, job_id, "in_progress")
   353	
   354	    return Dispatch(job_id=job_id, run_id=run_id, seat_id=builder_seat)
   355	
   356	
   357	def _spawn(seat_row, request: str, blocking: bool) -> str | None:
   358	    """Hand the work to OpenClaw.
   359	
   360	    `openclaw agent` runs one turn via the Gateway. The model is chosen per-spawn,
   361	    which is what lets a single orchestrator turn fan out across several families.
   362	    """
   363	    cmd = [
   364	        "openclaw", "agent",
   365	        "--model", f"{seat_row['provider']}/{seat_row['model']}",
   366	        "--message", request,
   367	    ]
   368	    if not blocking:
   369	        cmd.append("--detach")
   370	
     1	"""The worker. This is the thing that was missing — the reason nothing ran.
     2	
     3	Dispatch (dispatch.py) decides a job MAY run and records it. This module is what
     4	actually DOES it: it takes a queued job, runs the work on the assigned seat's real
     5	model, writes down every step as it goes, and lands a real result. Before this file
     6	existed, Chief said "putting Riggs on it" and no worker ever started.
     7	
     8	Two hard rules, both structural rather than polite:
     9	
    10	  1. THE WORK HAPPENS IN AN ISOLATED WORKTREE, never the live project. A builder gets
    11	     its own private copy of the repo (a git worktree). It physically cannot scribble
    12	     on main while it works — main is untouched until something is reviewed and merged
    13	     by the one service allowed to merge. "One worktree per agent" (owner + Sol).
    14	
    15	  2. LOCAL OUTPUT CANNOT COMPLETE ON ITS OWN. When the free local model finishes, the
    16	     job parks at 'review'. The database guards (schema.sql) refuse to let it reach
    17	     'done' without a higher-tier reviewer passing THIS version. We do not route
    18	     around that here; we feed it.
    19	
    20	Runs in a background thread so the app never blocks — the whole point of the
    21	architecture. sessions come and go; the job row is the durable truth.
    22	"""
    23	
    24	from __future__ import annotations
    25	
    26	import hashlib
    27	import os
    28	import json
    29	import subprocess
    30	import threading
    31	import urllib.request
    32	from pathlib import Path
    33	from typing import Any
    34	
    35	from db.jobs import (
    36	    connect,
    37	    seat,
    38	    set_head_version,
    39	    set_status,
    40	)
    41	
    42	HARNESS = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
    43	DB_PATH = HARNESS / "db" / "chief.db"
    44	WORKTREES = HARNESS / ".worktrees"       # gitignored; one subdir per job
    45	OUTPUT_DIRNAME = "chief_output"          # where a builder drops standalone work
    46	
    47	OLLAMA_URL = "http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/generate"
    48	
    49	
    50	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    51	# Event trail — this is what the app's activity view reads. Every step, live.
    52	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    53	def _event(conn, job_id: int, seat_row, kind: str, detail: str = "", target: str = "") -> None:
    54	    conn.execute(
    55	        "INSERT INTO events (job_id, seat_id, lane, model, family, kind, target, detail) "
    56	        "VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)",
    57	        (job_id, seat_row["id"], seat_row["id"], seat_row["model"],
    58	         seat_row["family"], kind, target or None, detail or None),
    59	    )
    60	
    61	
    62	def _commit_in_worktree(wt: Path, job_id: int, branch: str, summary: str) -> str | None:
    63	    """Commit the job's output on its own branch and return the commit id.
    64	
    65	    This is what makes the gatekeeper's merge possible at all: it refuses unless the
    66	    branch tip IS the reviewed version, and a version has to be a commit for that to be
    67	    provable. If anything here fails, the caller falls back to a content hash — the job
    68	    is still reviewable, it just can never be merged by the gatekeeper, which is the
    69	    honest outcome for work that isn't in git.
    70	    """
    71	    env = {**os.environ,
    72	           "GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "chief-worker", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "worker@chief.local",
    73	           "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "chief-worker", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "worker@chief.local"}
    74	    def git(*args):
    75	        return subprocess.run(["git", *args], cwd=wt, capture_output=True, text=True,
    76	                              timeout=60, env=env)
    77	    if git("rev-parse", "--is-inside-work-tree").stdout.strip() != "true":
    78	        return None
    79	    if git("checkout", "-B", branch).returncode != 0:
    80	        return None
    81	    if git("add", "-A", OUTPUT_DIRNAME).returncode != 0:
    82	        return None
    83	    if git("commit", "-q", "-m", f"job {job_id}: {summary[:60]}").returncode != 0:
    84	        return None
    85	    sha = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    86	    return sha[:16] if len(sha) >= 16 else None
    87	
    88	
    89	def _version_of(text: str) -> str:
    90	    """The exact content being put forward, as a short hash. This is what the whole
    91	    review-to-version chain binds to: change the output, change the version, and every
    92	    earlier approval silently stops counting (schema.sql, gate 3)."""
    93	    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16]
    94	
    95	
    96	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    97	# Worktree isolation — a builder never touches the live project.
    98	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    99	def _make_worktree(job_id: int, branch: str) -> tuple[Path | None, str]:
   100	    """Give this job its own private copy of the repo. Returns (path, note).
   101	
   102	    If git worktrees aren't usable for any reason, fall back to a plain isolated
   103	    directory and SAY SO — a silent fallback that quietly drops isolation would be
   104	    exactly the kind of unspoken gap this project keeps getting bitten by.
   105	    """
   106	    WORKTREES.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
   107	    dest = WORKTREES / f"job-{job_id}"
   108	    if dest.exists():
   109	        return dest, "reused"
   110	    try:
   111	        subprocess.run(
   112	            ["git", "worktree", "add", "--detach", str(dest)],
   113	            cwd=HARNESS.parent, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, check=True,
   114	        )
   115	        return dest, "worktree"
   116	    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
   117	        # Isolation still holds (separate dir, outside the repo tree) — it just isn't a
   118	        # git worktree, so we record that honestly.
   119	        dest.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
   120	        detail = getattr(exc, "stderr", "") or str(exc)
   121	        return dest, f"plain-dir ({str(detail)[:60].strip()})"
   122	
   123	
   124	def cleanup_worktree(job_id: int) -> None:
   125	    """Remove a job's worktree once its work is merged or abandoned."""
   126	    dest = WORKTREES / f"job-{job_id}"
   127	    if not dest.exists():
   128	        return
   129	    subprocess.run(["git", "worktree", "remove", "--force", str(dest)],
   130	                   cwd=HARNESS.parent, capture_output=True, text=True)
   131	    if dest.exists():
   132	        subprocess.run(["rm", "-rf", str(dest)], capture_output=True, text=True)
   133	
   134	
   135	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   136	# The actual builders, one per provider. Add a provider = add a function.
   137	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   138	def _ollama_build(seat_row, request: str, model: str) -> str:
   139	    """The free local coder actually writes the code."""
   140	    payload = json.dumps({"model": model, "prompt": request, "stream": False}).encode()
   141	    req = urllib.request.Request(
   142	        OLLAMA_URL, data=payload, headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"}
   143	    )
   144	    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=300) as r:
   145	        return json.loads(r.read())["response"].strip()
   146	
   147	
   148	_BUILDERS = {
   149	    "ollama": _ollama_build,
   150	}
   151	
   152	
   153	# Reviewing lives in gauntlet.py (task #10). This module builds; that one judges.
   154	# It used to hold a single hard-wired Claude reviewer, which was enough to close the
   155	# loop and was never the design — one reviewer cannot show that a DIFFERENT MIND looked.
   156	
   157	
   158	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   159	# The run loop — everything above, in order, for one job.
   160	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   161	def run_job(job_id: int, *, cfg: dict[str, Any] | None = None) -> dict[str, Any]:
   162	    """Take one recorded job and actually do it. Safe to call in a thread.
   163	
   164	    Opens its own database connection (sqlite + threads). WAL mode lets this write
   165	    while the web server reads. Never raises to the caller — a worker that dies must
   166	    leave a legible 'failed' row, not a stack trace nobody sees.
   167	    """
   168	    conn = connect(DB_PATH)
   169	    try:
   170	        job = conn.execute("SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE id = ?", (job_id,)).fetchone()
   171	        if job is None:
   172	            return {"job_id": job_id, "status": "missing"}
   173	        seat_row = seat(conn, job["builder_seat"])
   174	        if seat_row is None:
   175	            set_status(conn, job_id, "failed", error=f"unknown seat {job['builder_seat']}")
   176	            return {"job_id": job_id, "status": "failed"}
   177	
   178	        builder = _BUILDERS.get(seat_row["provider"])
   179	        if builder is None:
   180	            # A provider we don't have a local runner for (codex/xai/claude-cli builds
   181	            # go through OpenClaw, not this in-process worker). Say so plainly.
   182	            set_status(conn, job_id, "failed",
   183	                       error=f"no in-process builder for provider '{seat_row['provider']}' "
   184	                             "— this seat dispatches through OpenClaw")
   185	            _event(conn, job_id, seat_row, "error",
   186	                   "This worker only runs the local model directly.")
   187	            return {"job_id": job_id, "status": "failed"}
   188	
   189	        # 1. Isolate.
   190	        wt, wt_note = _make_worktree(job_id, job["branch"] or f"job/{job_id}")
   191	        conn.execute("UPDATE jobs SET worktree = ? WHERE id = ?", (str(wt), job_id))
   192	        _event(conn, job_id, seat_row, "dispatched", f"Working in its own copy ({wt_note}).")
   193	
   194	        # 2. Build.
   195	        model = job["tier"] and _model_for(seat_row, job["tier"]) or seat_row["model"]
   196	        _event(conn, job_id, seat_row, "thinking", "Working out how to do it.")
   197	        try:
   198	            result = builder(seat_row, job["request"], model)
   199	        except Exception as exc:  # network, model, timeout — all land here
   200	            set_status(conn, job_id, "failed", error=f"builder error: {exc}")
   201	            _event(conn, job_id, seat_row, "error", "The worker hit a problem.")
   202	            return {"job_id": job_id, "status": "failed"}
   203	
   204	        # 3. Land the output in the isolated copy, and version it.
   205	        out_dir = wt / OUTPUT_DIRNAME
   206	        out_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
   207	        (out_dir / f"job_{job_id}.txt").write_text(result)
   208	        branch = job["branch"] or f"job/{job_id}"
   209	        version = _commit_in_worktree(wt, job_id, branch, job["request"]) or _version_of(result)
   210	        set_head_version(conn, job_id, version)
   211	        _event(conn, job_id, seat_row, "write",
   212	               f"Wrote {len(result)} characters of work.", target=str(out_dir))
   213	
   214	        # 4. Park for review. The guards will hold it here until a real reviewer passes
   215	        #    THIS version — we never force it past them.
   216	        set_status(conn, job_id, "review", result=result)
   217	
   218	        # 5. Hand the frozen bundle to the review panel — several families at once,
   219	        #    all bound to THIS version. The panel decides nothing the database wouldn't;
   220	        #    it produces the verdicts the guards ask for. Without a config there is no
   221	        #    panel, and the job simply stays parked (the safe outcome, not a shortcut).
   222	        if cfg is not None:
   223	            import gauntlet
   224	            gauntlet.run_panel(conn, job_id, job["request"], result, version, cfg,
   225	                               db_path=DB_PATH)
   226	
   227	        final = conn.execute("SELECT status FROM jobs WHERE id = ?", (job_id,)).fetchone()["status"]
   228	        return {"job_id": job_id, "status": final, "version": version}
   229	    finally:
   230	        conn.close()
   231	
   232	
   233	def _model_for(seat_row, tier: str) -> str:
   234	    try:
   235	        from tiering import resolve_model
   236	        return resolve_model(seat_row, tier)[0]
   237	    except Exception:
   238	        return seat_row["model"]
   239	
   240	
   241	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   242	# Fire-and-forget: start a worker in the background so the app stays responsive.
   243	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   244	def start_in_background(job_id: int, *, cfg: dict[str, Any] | None = None) -> threading.Thread:
   245	    t = threading.Thread(
   246	        target=run_job, args=(job_id,), kwargs={"cfg": cfg},
   247	        name=f"job-{job_id}", daemon=True,
   248	    )
   249	    t.start()
   250	    return t

