     1	# Chief Command v2 — Phase 1 Harness
     2	
     3	The text harness. Voice is a swappable skin over this, and this works fully without it —
     4	which is the one thing the spec got exactly right, and the reason v1's voice problem
     5	could never happen again here.
     6	
     7	**Phase 1 acceptance (spec §8):** from the phone, type *"have the local model write X,
     8	then have Codex review it"* and it happens, with the job recorded.
     9	
    10	---
    11	
    12	## What's here
    13	
    14	| Path | What it is |
    15	|---|---|
    16	| `db/schema.sql` | Job store. **The two structural guards live here** (see below). |
    17	| `db/jobs.py` | Job store API — create, atomic-claim, verdicts, budgets, reporting. |
    18	| `config/seats.toml` | The named roles from §4. Providers swap behind these; nothing else references a provider by name. |
    19	| `tests/test_jobs.py` | 14 tests. The guard tests are the load-bearing ones. |
    20	
    21	## Why this exists at all
    22	
    23	Most of the spec is already OpenClaw: the four seats are `agents.list[]`, the dispatcher
    24	is `sessions_spawn` (non-blocking, per-spawn model override), the gauntlet is the stock
    25	`autoreview` skill, and the tailnet + launchd daemon ship in core. **Rebuilding those
    26	would have been the mistake.**
    27	
    28	Three things core does *not* give us, and they're what this directory is:
    29	
    30	1. **Durable job history.** OpenClaw's subagent sessions auto-archive after ~60 minutes
    31	   and get soft-deleted. That's session archival, not the queryable build history §7
    32	   wants ("what did the overnight run do?").
    33	2. **Per-seat budget caps.** Core has no per-agent cost or token budget of any kind.
    34	3. **The never-ship-unreviewed rule**, enforced structurally.
    35	
    36	## The guards
    37	
    38	§9 says to enforce the local-output rule *"in the pipeline itself, not by convention."*
    39	That means the database, not the prompt. **Rules that live in prompts quietly stop
    40	holding at 4am.**
    41	
    42	- **Guard 1 — local output never ships unreviewed.** A job built by a `local`-tier seat
    43	  cannot reach `done` without a *passing* verdict from a higher tier. Another local seat
    44	  can't rescue it (two juniors are not a senior). A failing review can't rescue it.
    45	  Re-tiering a seat after the fact can't retroactively legitimize it — `reviewer_tier`
    46	  is snapshotted at write time.
    47	- **Guard 2 — escalations must be answered, not outrun.** An unresolved `needs_human`
    48	  verdict blocks completion.
    49	
    50	Both are SQLite triggers. The write simply fails. `tests/test_jobs.py` proves it.
    51	
    52	---
    53	
    54	## Setup
    55	
    56	### Already done on this machine
    57	- ✅ OpenClaw **pinned to 2026.6.11** (no `latest` — there were three breaking releases
    58	  in under 90 days in Q1 2026; version bumps are deliberate, tested events)
    59	- ✅ Ollama installed + serving on `127.0.0.1:11434`
    60	- ✅ `qwen2.5-coder:7b` pulled — **sized for this box, see below**
    61	- ✅ Job store + guards + tests
    62	
    63	### ⚠️ Hardware reality (Phase 0 RAM check, §2)
    64	**This machine is an M2 Pro with 16 GB.** The spec's 30B-class local model wants 36 GB+.
    65	So the grinder is a **7B coder** — a genuinely junior dev. That's survivable precisely
    66	because Guard 1 makes it *impossible* to ship its output unreviewed. Bump to
    67	`qwen3-coder:30b` on the Studio (§8 Phase 5) by changing one line in `seats.toml`.
    68	
    69	### Steps that need you (interactive auth — I can't do these)
    70	
    71	```bash
    72	# 1. Tailscale — the harness binds to the tailnet, never the public internet (§2)
    73	brew install --cask tailscale
    74	# then sign in via the app, and:
    75	tailscale status
    76	
    77	# 2. Codex OAuth (orchestrator seat)
    78	npm install -g @openai/codex
    79	codex login          # opens a browser; uses your ChatGPT subscription
    80	
    81	# 3. Grok (workhorse seat) — ⚠️ TEST THE TIER FIRST, see below
    82	openclaw configure   # walk the provider setup
    83	
    84	# 4. Gateway on the tailnet + launchd
    85	openclaw gateway install
    86	openclaw gateway --bind tailnet
    87	```
    88	
    89	### 🚩 Test the Grok tier before you count on it
    90	xAI's launch said Grok Build is available to all SuperGrok subscribers, but there's a
    91	live bug report that OAuth **403s for standard SuperGrok ($30/mo)** with the backend
    92	apparently gated to SuperGrok **Heavy ($300/mo)**. Cheap test, expensive assumption.
    93	If it 403s, the workhorse seat falls back to the orchestrator or the grinder.
    94	
    95	---
    96	
    97	## Things the spec got wrong (corrected in `seats.toml`)
    98	
    99	Full detail in `docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md`. The load-bearing ones:
   100	
   101	- **The Claude credit pool does not exist.** Anthropic announced it, then *paused it
   102	  before it took effect*. `claude -p` draws on normal subscription limits today. The cap
   103	  in `seats.toml` is forward-proofing, not a current necessity.
   104	- **Codex parallel workers: 6, not 8.** `max_threads` defaults to 6.
   105	- **Codex OAuth is tolerated, not sanctioned.** No OpenAI doc blesses it, and both other
   106	  major vendors closed this exact door within five months. Keep the API-key swap ready —
   107	  the provider abstraction makes that nearly free.
   108	- **The voice head is a new seat the spec didn't have.** §5.1 had the voice layer talking
   109	  straight to the orchestrator. GPT-5.6-sol measures **~13s to first token** against a
   110	  1.5s budget. The head is fast and deliberately not smart; the orchestrator becomes a
   111	  dispatch target whose latency is invisible because you're never waiting on it.
   112	
   113	## Run the tests
   114	
   115	```bash
   116	.venv/bin/python -m pytest harness/tests/ -q
   117	```
     1	"""The live database must carry the same guards the tests test.
     2	
     3	Every other test in this suite builds a FRESH database from schema.sql and proves
     4	the guards hold there. None of that says anything about harness/db/chief.db — and
     5	on 2026-07-20 the live DB turned out to be missing two guards (verdict deletion,
     6	born-granted approvals) and running a stale panel guard, months of green tests
     7	notwithstanding. Sol's build gate 1: the live DB must match the hardened rules.
     8	
     9	These tests skip cleanly when no live DB exists (fresh checkout, CI).
    10	"""
    11	
    12	import sqlite3
    13	from pathlib import Path
    14	
    15	import pytest
    16	
    17	HARNESS = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
    18	SCHEMA = HARNESS / "db" / "schema.sql"
    19	LIVE_DB = HARNESS / "db" / "chief.db"
    20	
    21	pytestmark = pytest.mark.skipif(
    22	    not LIVE_DB.exists(), reason="no live database on this machine"
    23	)
    24	
    25	
    26	def _object_names(conn: sqlite3.Connection, kind: str) -> set[str]:
    27	    rows = conn.execute(
    28	        "SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = ? AND name NOT LIKE 'sqlite_%'",
    29	        (kind,),
    30	    )
    31	    return {r[0] for r in rows}
    32	
    33	
    34	def _columns(conn: sqlite3.Connection, table: str) -> set[tuple]:
    35	    # (name, type, notnull, default) — column ORDER is allowed to differ, because
    36	    # ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN appends and that is how live migrations happen.
    37	    rows = conn.execute(f"PRAGMA table_info({table})")
    38	    return {(r[1], r[2], r[3], r[4]) for r in rows}
    39	
    40	
    41	def _normalized_trigger_sql(conn: sqlite3.Connection) -> dict[str, str]:
    42	    rows = conn.execute("SELECT name, sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'trigger'")
    43	    # Whitespace and comments may drift between schema.sql and a migration; the
    44	    # executable text must not. Strip comment lines, collapse whitespace.
    45	    out = {}
    46	    for name, sql in rows:
    47	        lines = [
    48	            line.split("--")[0] for line in sql.splitlines()
    49	        ]
    50	        out[name] = " ".join(" ".join(lines).split())
    51	    return out
    52	
    53	
    54	@pytest.fixture(scope="module")
    55	def fresh():
    56	    conn = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")
    57	    conn.executescript(SCHEMA.read_text())
    58	    yield conn
    59	    conn.close()
    60	
    61	
    62	@pytest.fixture(scope="module")
    63	def live():
    64	    # Read-only: this test must never be able to touch the record.
    65	    conn = sqlite3.connect(f"file:{LIVE_DB}?mode=ro", uri=True)
    66	    yield conn
    67	    conn.close()
    68	
    69	
    70	def test_live_db_has_every_table(fresh, live):
    71	    assert _object_names(fresh, "table") <= _object_names(live, "table")
    72	
    73	
    74	def test_live_db_has_every_view(fresh, live):
    75	    assert _object_names(fresh, "view") <= _object_names(live, "view")
    76	
    77	
    78	def test_live_db_has_every_guard_with_identical_teeth(fresh, live):
    79	    fresh_triggers = _normalized_trigger_sql(fresh)
    80	    live_triggers = _normalized_trigger_sql(live)
    81	    missing = set(fresh_triggers) - set(live_triggers)
    82	    assert not missing, f"live DB is missing guards: {sorted(missing)}"
    83	    stale = {
    84	        name
    85	        for name, sql in fresh_triggers.items()
    86	        if live_triggers[name] != sql
    87	    }
    88	    assert not stale, f"live DB guards differ from schema.sql: {sorted(stale)}"
    89	
    90	
    91	def test_live_db_tables_have_every_column(fresh, live):
    92	    for table in _object_names(fresh, "table"):
    93	        missing = _columns(fresh, table) - _columns(live, table)
    94	        assert not missing, f"live {table} is missing columns: {sorted(missing)}"
